  "evidence": "Text A (Labour): secure borders and a New Border Security Command; reduce net migration with skills-linked visas and tougher enforcement; Neighbourhood Policing Guarantee with thousands more officers/PCSOs; new Respect Orders; specific offence for assaults on shopworkers and tougher shoplifting rules; mandatory interventions on knife crime with bans on machetes/zombie knives and online sales; expand prison capacity; higher policing standards and vetting; unshakeable NATO and nuclear deterrent; Strategic Defence Review toward 2.5% of GDP; defence industrial strategy; steadfast support for Ukraine; pragmatic China policy. Text B (Liberal Democrats): restore community policing and guarantee attendance/investigation of burglaries; defence target 2.5% of GDP and maintain nuclear deterrent; but also scrap anti‑protest laws; end disproportionate Stop & Search; scrap Police and Crime Commissioners; end Hostile Environment, scrap Illegal Migration Act and Rwanda plan; faster, more permissive asylum processing and expanded safe/legal routes; protect the Human Rights Act.",
  "reasoning": "The profile prioritises Authority, and Text A places stronger emphasis on hierarchical order and enforcement through tougher border controls, expanded policing, new offences and bans, prison capacity, and firm commitments to NATO and defence. While Text B contains some authority-affirming elements, it simultaneously rolls back enforcement and constraints (anti-protest laws, stop and search, migration controls), making it less aligned with an Authority-focused profile.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "A"
}
